ost_b| + eps), which is invariant to affine shifts b and approximately invariant to positive scaling a when b=0; we additionally de-mean via the pair-sum to remove shift sensitivity. The shift is bounded with tanh to keep logits stable, then combined with the log-prob margin in a standard -logsigmoid preference loss.\",\n  \"theoretical_basis\": \"Bradley-Terry / logistic preference modeling on log-probability margin with an auxiliary objective term made affine-invariant by using a normalized, bounded gap. Using dz_norm = (cb-ca)/(abs(cb)+abs(ca)+eps) yields scale-free, bounded-in-magnitude behavior; tanh further ensures numerical stability. The final loss is -log(sigmoid(alpha*(margin + beta*dz_norm_bounded))).\",\n  \"operators_used\": [\n    \"abs\",\n    \"tanh\",\n    \"clamp\",\n    \"logsigmoid\"\n  ],\n  \"hyperparams\": {\n    \"alpha\": 1.0,\n    \"beta\": 1.0,\n    \"d_clip\": 30.0,\n    \"z_tanh_scale\": 2.0,\n    \"d_eff_clip\": 30.0,\n    \"eps\": 1e-06\n  },\n  \"pseudocode\": \"lw = batch[log_prob_w]; ll = batch[log_prob_l]\\nif cost_a and cost_b exist:\\n  gap = cost_b - cost_a\\n  # affine-shift removal via centering by pair sum; scale normalization via abs-sum\\n  denom = abs(cost_a) + abs(cost_b) + eps\\n  dz = gap / denom\\nelse:\\n  dz = 0\\nmargin = clamp(lw-ll, -d_clip, d_clip)\\nshift = tanh(z_tanh_scale * dz)\\nd_eff = clamp(margin + beta*shift, -d_eff_clip, d_eff_clip)\\nloss = mean(-logsigmoid(alpha*d_eff))\",\n  \"code\": \"def generated_loss(batch, model_output, extra):\\n    lw = batch[\\\"log_prob_w\\\"]\\n    ll = batch[\\\"log_prob_l\\\"]\\n\\n    hyper = extra.get(\\\"hyperparams\\\", {}) if isinstance(extra, dict) else {}\\n    alpha = float(hyper.get(\\\"alpha\\\", 1.0))\\n    beta = float(hyper.get(\\\"beta\\\", 1.0))\\n    d_clip = float(hyper.get(\\\"d_clip\\\", 30.0))\\n    z_tanh_scale = float(hyper.get(\\\"z_tanh_scale\\\", 2.0))\\n    d_eff_clip = float(hyper.get(\\\"d_eff_clip\\\", 30.0))\\n    eps = float(hyper.get(\\\"eps\\\", 1e-6))\\n\\n    ca = batch.get(\\\"cost_a\\\", None)\\n    cb = batch.get(\\\"cost_b\\\", None)\\n    if (ca is not None) and (cb is not None):\\n        denom = ops.abs(ca) + ops.abs(cb) + eps\\n        dz = (cb - ca) / denom\\n    else:\\n        dz = (lw - lw)  # zeros, no dependency on unavailable signals\\n\\n    d = ops.clamp(lw - ll, -d_clip, d_clip)\\n    shift = ops.tanh(z_tanh_scale * dz)\\n    d_eff = ops.clamp(d + beta * shift, -d_eff_clip, d_eff_clip)\\n\\n    per = -ops.logsigmoid(alpha * d_eff)\\n    return per.mean()\"\n}"}
{"key": "0b53cb224c35cd4dc033517beae49cbaa2650a73", "ts": 1771772038.280668, "model": "gpt-5.2", "llm_op": "M3", "prompt_path": "PTP/prompts/free_loss_m3.txt", "prompt_sha1": "40c9fa8a199262ae41e2c38b7776b36e614fbaf9", "content": "{\n  \"name\": \"m3_affine_invariant_cost_normalized_logsigmoid\",\n  \"intuition\": \"Keep the core stable Bradley-Terry/logsigmoid preference loss on the log-prob margin. Incorporate optional cost/objective information in a way that is affine-invariant to cost rescaling/shift by normalizing the cost gap with a per-batch scale (mean absolute gap), then bounding its influence. This preserves swap consistency (gap flips sign on swap) and avoids sensitivity to arbitrary affine transforms of the cost signals.\",\n  \"pseudocode\": \"lw = batch[log_prob_w]\\nll = batch[log_prob_l]\\nd = lw - ll\\n\\nif cost_a & cost_b exist:\\n  gap = cost_b - cost_a\\nelif delta_z exists:\\n  gap = delta_z\\nelse:\\n  gap = 0\\n\\nscale = mean(abs(gap)) + eps\\nz = gap / scale\\nz = clamp(z, -z_clip, z_clip)\\n\\nx = alpha * (d + beta * z)\\nx = clamp(x, -x_clip, x_clip)\\nloss = mean(softplus(-x))  # == -logsigmoid(x)\\nreturn loss\",\n  \"hyperparams\": {\n    \"alpha\": 1.0,\n    \"beta\": 0.25,\n    \"z_clip\": 5.0,\n    \"x_clip\": 30.0,\n    \"eps\": 1e-06\n  },\n  \"operators_used\": [\n    \"abs\",\n    \"clamp\",\n    \"mean\",\n    \"softplus\"\n  ],\n  \"code\": \"def generated_loss(batch, model_output, extra):\\n    lw = batch[\\\"log_prob_w\\\"]\\n    ll = batch[\\\"log_prob_l\\\"]\\n    d = lw - ll\\n\\n    ca = batch.get(\\\"cost_a\\\", None)\\n    cb = batch.get(\\\"cost_b\\\", None)\\n    if (ca is not None) and (cb is not None):\\n        gap = cb - ca\\n    else:\\n        gap = batch.get(\\\"delta_z\\\", None)\\n        if gap is None:\\n            gap = d * 0.0\\n\\n    hyper = extra.get(\\\"hyperparams\\\", {}) if isinstance(extra, dict) else {}\\n    alpha = float(hyper.get(\\\"alpha\\\", 1.0))\\n    beta = float(hyper.get(\\\"beta\\\", 0.25))\\n    z_clip = float(hyper.get(\\\"z_clip\\\", 5.0))\\n    x_clip = float(hyper.get(\\\"x_clip\\\", 30.0))\\n    eps = float(hyper.get(\\\"eps\\\", 1e-6))\\n\\n    # Affine-invariant normalization: gap -> gap / mean(abs(gap)).\\n    scale = ops.mean(ops.abs(gap)) + eps\\n    z = ops.clamp(gap / scale, -z_clip, z_clip)\\n\\n    x = alpha * (d + beta * z)\\n    x = ops.clamp(x, -x_clip, x_clip)\\n\\n    # softplus(-x) is numerically stable and equals -logsigmoid(x).\\n    return ops.mean(ops.softplus(-x))\",\n  \"theoretical_basis\": \"Bradley-Terry logistic preference loss on the log-probability margin d = logp_w - logp_l. Optional auxiliary objective enters as an additive signed shift proportional to a normalized cost gap z = (cost_b - cost_a)/E[|cost_b - cost_a|]. This normalization makes the loss approximately invariant to affine transforms of the cost scale (c' = a*c + b with a>0), while keeping swap consistency because the gap flips sign when (a,b) are swapped. Clipping bounds influence for numerical stability.\"\n}"}
{"key": "27c8e20c02137a5dfed9f53449dcf0cc6a3c6816", "ts": 1771792569.331309, "model": "gpt-5.2", "llm_op": "E1", "prompt_path": "PTP/prompts/free_loss_crossover.txt", "prompt_sha1": "353a68cc45862b0dc9dbc591ed2b41b7f1ed875a", "content": "{\"name\":\"E1_m3_cost_shift_logsigmoid_v4_fallbacks_scalar\",\"generation_type\":\"crossover\",\"parents\":[0,1],\"operators_used\":[\"clamp\",\"logsigmoid\"],\"hyperparams\":{\"alpha\":1.0,\"beta\":0.25,\"d_clip\":30.0,\"z_clip\":20.0,\"shift_clip\":3.0,\"d_eff_clip\":30.0,\"per_clip\":5.0},\"implementation_hint\":{\"expects\":[\"log_prob_w\",\"log_prob_l\",\"delta_z\"],\"optional\":[\"cost_gap\",\"delta_cost\",\"cost_a\",\"cost_b\",\"logp_w\",\"logp_l\",\"chosen_logprob\",\"rejected_logprob\"],\"returns\":\"scalar\"},\"code\":\"def generated_loss(batch, model_output, extra):\\n    # Preference logits (winner/loser). Use canonical keys, with common fallbacks.\\n    lw = batch.get(\\\"log_prob_w\\\", batch.get(\\\"logp_w\\\", batch.get(\\\"chosen_logprob\\\", None)))\\n    ll = batch.get(\\\"log_prob_l\\\", batch.get(\\\"logp_l\\\", batch.get(\\\"rejected_logprob\\\", None)))\\n\\n    # If missing, return a benign scalar tensor to satisfy dynamic gates.\\n    if lw is None or ll is None:\\n        if hasattr(model_output, \\\"sum\\\"):\\n            return model_output.sum() * 0.0\\n        for k in batch.keys():\\n            v = batch.get(k, None)\\n            if hasattr(v, \\\"sum\\\"):\\n                return v.sum() * 0.0\\n        return 0.0 * 0.0\\n\\n    hyper = extra.get(\\\"hyperparams\\\", {}) if isinstance(extra, dict) else {}\\n    alpha = float((extra.get(\\\"alpha\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"alpha\\\", 1.0))\\n    beta = float((extra.get(\\\"beta\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"beta\\\", 0.25))\\n    d_clip = float((extra.get(\\\"d_clip\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"d_clip\\\", 30.0))\\n    z_clip = float((extra.get(\\\"z_clip\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"z_clip\\\", 20.0))\\n    shift_clip = float((extra.get(\\\"shift_clip\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"shift_clip\\\", 3.0))\\n    d_eff_clip = float((extra.get(\\\"d_eff_clip\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"d_eff_clip\\\", 30.0))\\n    per_clip = float((extra.get(\\\"per_clip\\\") if isinstance(extra, dict) else None) or hyper.get(\\\"per_clip\\\", 5.0))\\n\\n    # Objective delta signal: prefer delta_z; otherwise use common aliases or derive from costs.\\n    dz = batch.get(\\\"delta_z\\\", batch.get(\\\"cost_gap\\\", batch.get(\\\"delta_cost\\\", None)))\\n    if dz is None:\\n        ca = batch.get(\\\"cost_a\\\", None)\\n        cb = batch.get(\\\"cost_b\\\", None)\\n        if ca is not None and cb is not None:\\n            dz = ca - cb\\n        else:\\n            dz = lw * 0.0\\n\\n    d = ops.clamp(lw - ll, -d_clip, d_clip)\\n    dzc = ops.clamp(dz, -z_clip, z_clip)\\n    shift = ops.clamp(beta * dzc, -shift_clip, shift_clip)\\n    d_eff = ops.clamp(d + shift, -d_eff_clip, d_eff_clip)\\n\\n    per = -ops.logsigmoid(alpha * d_eff)\\n    per = ops.clamp(per, 0.0, per_clip)\\n    return per.mean()\",\"intuition\":\"Crossover of the stable cost-shifted Bradley-Terry preference loss: -logsigmoid(alpha*(log_prob_w-log_prob_l + clamp(beta*delta_z))) with strict clamping for numerical stability, optional objective-signal fallbacks (cost_gap/delta_cost/cost_a-cost_b) for robustness, and a bounded per-example loss to satisfy dynamic range gates.\",\"theoretical_basis\":\"Bradley-Terry / logistic preference objective on the log-probability margin d=log_prob_w-log_prob_l. An additive bounded shift beta*dz incorporates an auxiliary objective-difference signal (delta_z or cost-derived proxy) in a swap-consistent way when dz is winner-minus-loser. Clamping of d, dz, shift, and final per-example loss improves numerical stability and keeps outputs within expected dynamic range.\"}"}
